WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:05.120
Normally in projects we have a database and we have a person that is responsible for the database,

00:05.120 --> 00:09.200
the database administrator that takes cares of the database structure.

00:09.240 --> 00:14.640
And now in very simple scenario, we're going to have a user that is writing queries in order to retrieve

00:14.680 --> 00:15.960
data from the database.

00:15.960 --> 00:18.440
So he or she can write an SQL query.

00:18.440 --> 00:21.920
And then this query is going to be sent to the database where it's going to execute it.

00:21.920 --> 00:24.640
And then the database can return the results.

00:24.680 --> 00:29.240
So at the end our user is going to see the result of the query that he wrote.

00:29.240 --> 00:34.440
So this is a very simplified scenario on how we use a database.

00:34.440 --> 00:38.040
But my friends in the real world things are totally different.

00:38.080 --> 00:40.800
Things in real projects get very complicated.

00:40.800 --> 00:41.560
Like this.

00:41.560 --> 00:49.360
So for example, you have a financial analyst that is writing a huge block of SQL query that is very

00:49.360 --> 00:54.600
complex, and there will be like another user that have different role like a risk manager.

00:54.600 --> 01:01.330
That is, as we're writing a very complex query and from different departments, from different projects,

01:01.330 --> 01:02.410
for different tasks.

01:02.410 --> 01:07.730
You will have a lot of analysts that are writing many complex queries.

01:07.730 --> 01:13.770
So all those analysts and managers have a direct access to your database, and they are executing a

01:13.770 --> 01:18.130
complex analytical queries in order to generate maybe a report or something.

01:18.170 --> 01:24.130
Now, not only those guys are doing analysis on your database, you will have as well our friend, the

01:24.130 --> 01:29.090
data engineer that is saying, you know what, I'm building a data warehouse and I would like to extract

01:29.090 --> 01:35.090
your data so that data engineering can go and write and extract query in order to extract the data from

01:35.090 --> 01:35.970
the database.

01:35.970 --> 01:41.890
And then he has a different script for the transformations in order to manipulate, filter, clean up

01:41.930 --> 01:43.370
aggregates your data.

01:43.410 --> 01:50.170
And then a third script in order to collect the result of the transformations and load it in another

01:50.170 --> 01:52.290
database called data warehouse.

01:52.330 --> 01:57.780
A data warehouse is like a special database that collect data from different sources and integrated

01:57.780 --> 02:00.940
in one place in order to do analytics and reporting.

02:00.980 --> 02:06.780
And now at the end of this chain, you will have a data analyst and she writes as well queries in order

02:06.780 --> 02:08.940
to analyze the data in the data warehouse.

02:08.940 --> 02:14.500
Or you might have a different query in order to prepare the data before inserting it to a tool like

02:14.500 --> 02:18.620
power BI in order to generate visualizations and reports.

02:18.740 --> 02:24.300
So we call this a data warehouse system, or a business intelligence system that extract an extra copy

02:24.300 --> 02:28.380
from your data and manipulate it and transform it for analysis.

02:28.420 --> 02:33.700
Now, not only we have a data engineer and data analyst accessing your database and doing queries.

02:33.740 --> 02:36.300
We have as well our friend, the data scientist.

02:36.300 --> 02:40.660
So now our data scientist as well has a direct access to your database.

02:40.660 --> 02:45.460
So he might write like different queries in order to extract the data and as well to manipulate the

02:45.460 --> 02:51.020
data that are needed in order to develop a model and doing machine learning and AI.

02:51.140 --> 02:56.360
And now one more scenario that I see in many projects where the result of the data, analyst can be

02:56.360 --> 03:00.640
used in another query in order to prepare the results for data visualizations.

03:00.640 --> 03:04.280
Power BI or in order to export like an Excel list.

03:04.280 --> 03:09.880
So as you can see, we have a lot of people with different roles that want to access your database and

03:09.880 --> 03:15.440
do analyses on top of it, and that's because everyone wants to answer questions based on the data.

03:15.480 --> 03:21.120
And now if I look to this, I still think this is a simplified version on how things works in the data

03:21.120 --> 03:21.640
projects.

03:21.640 --> 03:25.720
And I can tell you in real project things are way more complicated than this.

03:25.760 --> 03:30.640
So now if you sit back and look to this, we will find many challenges and problems.

03:30.640 --> 03:35.960
For example, all those people are not talking to each other and each one of them are creating like

03:36.000 --> 03:37.040
their own query.

03:37.040 --> 03:42.040
But if you go and take all those queries and compare them side by side, you will find in the scripts

03:42.040 --> 03:43.720
and queries logic.

03:43.720 --> 03:49.520
That is, keep repeating so the queries from the analyst or the data scientist and data engineers,

03:49.520 --> 03:52.760
they might contain a redundant logic.

03:52.760 --> 03:58.010
And of course the issue of this, we have the same effort repeating over and over.

03:58.050 --> 04:03.570
And maybe not everyone is getting the logic implemented correctly, because not all of them having the

04:03.570 --> 04:04.930
right skills in SQL.

04:05.050 --> 04:07.290
So this is a big issue in this setup.

04:07.330 --> 04:10.490
And now we have another challenge having this scenario.

04:10.490 --> 04:14.410
If you don't optimize it you will have a performance issue everywhere.

04:14.490 --> 04:19.050
So the data warehouse or the data engineer scripts might take like five hours.

04:19.050 --> 04:22.250
And the query from the analyst might take like 40 minutes.

04:22.290 --> 04:27.890
And before inserting the data to reports we might have 30 minutes and one hour there, 30 minutes there.

04:27.890 --> 04:31.930
And everyone else is as well suffering from bad performance on their queries.

04:31.930 --> 04:34.490
And the performance everywhere is really bad.

04:34.530 --> 04:39.730
So if everyone is writing big, complex queries, don't expect that they will have a good performance.

04:39.770 --> 04:45.370
Now to the third challenge that I observed in many projects, and that is the complexity now behind

04:45.370 --> 04:51.540
the original database, you might have a data model that is prepared and optimized only for one application.

04:51.540 --> 04:55.980
So you will have in the data model a lot of tables and all those tables have different relationship

04:55.980 --> 04:56.580
between them.

04:56.580 --> 05:02.660
And of course, only the developers and the experts of this database understand the physical data model

05:02.700 --> 05:04.220
behind this database.

05:04.260 --> 05:09.660
And now if you give access to all those analysts, they will have a lot of questions, because first

05:09.700 --> 05:13.820
they have to understand the data model before writing any query.

05:13.820 --> 05:19.940
So that means a lot of data workers are keep asking our experts from this database questions.

05:19.940 --> 05:22.620
So for example how to connect the table A with the table B.

05:22.660 --> 05:24.620
And where do I find my columns.

05:24.620 --> 05:30.060
What this table means I'm getting bad results in my query because your data is really corrupt.

05:30.060 --> 05:35.340
So the developers of the database will get a lot of questions from the analysts, and they have to explain

05:35.340 --> 05:41.420
over and over their data model so that the users are able to write those complex queries.

05:41.420 --> 05:46.020
So that means all those users are stressing the database team by many questions.

05:46.020 --> 05:49.550
And as well the users are writing very complex queries.

05:49.550 --> 05:52.670
So the complexity is a really big challenge now as well.

05:52.710 --> 05:57.390
By looking to this picture, you will find a lot of arrows from those queries to the database.

05:57.590 --> 06:00.990
And this might cause a lot of database stress.

06:00.990 --> 06:02.990
So keep executing repeatedly.

06:03.350 --> 06:07.910
A big complex queries can make really big stress for the database.

06:07.910 --> 06:09.950
And it's going to bring the database down.

06:09.950 --> 06:13.510
And the last challenge of this picture that the data security.

06:13.510 --> 06:19.030
So if you leave it like this, by giving the users a direct access to your database tables, you might

06:19.030 --> 06:23.350
have a problem because it might be okay for like some data engineers and so on.

06:23.350 --> 06:28.510
But you don't want to give for each data analyst a full access to the database tables.

06:28.510 --> 06:32.590
So you have to protect your tables, the columns, the rows, everything.

06:32.590 --> 06:38.430
So you cannot leave it like this where everyone having a direct access to the physical database tables.

06:38.470 --> 06:41.190
Now enough talking about challenges, problems and issues.

06:41.230 --> 06:43.270
Let's be solution oriented.

06:43.270 --> 06:45.590
So what are the solutions of those issues?

06:45.630 --> 06:49.800
Of course there are many solutions, but we're going to focus now on five techniques.

06:49.800 --> 06:53.960
We can go and use subqueries or ctes common table expressions.

06:53.960 --> 07:00.080
We can introduce views to our database or temporary tables, or we can go and use the technique of the

07:00.080 --> 07:03.240
CTAs create table as select.

07:03.240 --> 07:08.760
So this is exactly why we have to understand those five techniques in order to solve all those issues

07:08.760 --> 07:11.360
that we might face in our data projects.

07:15.200 --> 07:15.960
All right friends.

07:15.960 --> 07:21.880
So now after we understood the importance of those five techniques, let's take a quick and simplified

07:21.880 --> 07:27.320
look to the database architecture, because I want you to understand what happens behind the scenes

07:27.320 --> 07:32.520
and how the database executes the queries from these five techniques.

07:32.520 --> 07:36.640
So by understanding this architecture you will understand how things works.

07:36.640 --> 07:39.720
So let's go for each story there are two sides.

07:39.720 --> 07:42.240
We have the server side and the client side.

07:42.240 --> 07:48.210
In the client side it's like for example you you are writing an SQL query for a specific purpose.

07:48.210 --> 07:50.490
Now in the server side we have many things.

07:50.490 --> 07:56.410
So the server is where the database lives and it has many components like the database engine.

07:56.410 --> 08:04.330
The database engine is the brain of the database that handles different operations like storing, retrieving

08:04.330 --> 08:06.490
and managing data in the database.

08:06.490 --> 08:10.610
So each time you execute a query, the database engine can take care of it.

08:10.650 --> 08:14.690
And now in the database we have a very important component that is the storage.

08:14.690 --> 08:20.130
And the two main types of storage in a database are disk storage and cache.

08:20.170 --> 08:25.450
The disk storage is like a long term memory where the data is stored permanently.

08:25.450 --> 08:27.250
So it's like the disk at your PC.

08:27.410 --> 08:31.530
It stores the data permanently, even if you turn off the system.

08:31.530 --> 08:35.690
And one important feature of the disk is that it can store a lot of data.

08:35.690 --> 08:41.730
But the disadvantage of the disk storage is that it is slow, so it is slow to write and to read.

08:41.770 --> 08:47.550
Now in the other hand, we have the cash is a short term memory where the data is stored temporary.

08:47.550 --> 08:49.150
It's like the Rams at your PC.

08:49.190 --> 08:55.350
It holds the most frequently used data, so the database can access it quickly in order to retrieve

08:55.390 --> 08:55.830
data.

08:55.830 --> 08:58.870
And the big advantage of the cache is that it is fast.

08:58.870 --> 09:03.550
So it is very fast for the database to retrieve data from the cache compared to the disk.

09:03.550 --> 09:08.270
But the disadvantage of the cache, the data is stored there only for short periods.

09:08.270 --> 09:12.950
So it's like a trade off between the speed and how much data you can store and how long.

09:12.950 --> 09:14.790
Now let's talk about the disk storage.

09:14.790 --> 09:16.550
This is very important in databases.

09:16.590 --> 09:19.790
There are typically three types of storage areas.

09:19.790 --> 09:20.230
There.

09:20.230 --> 09:24.750
We have the user data the system catalog and the temporary data.

09:24.750 --> 09:27.470
And each storage type has a different purpose.

09:27.470 --> 09:29.510
So what is user data storage.

09:29.510 --> 09:32.190
It is the main content of the database.

09:32.190 --> 09:34.510
So it stores the actual data.

09:34.550 --> 09:38.230
All the informations that are relevant for the users.

09:38.230 --> 09:42.160
So it's stored there, all the important data that the users cares about.

09:42.160 --> 09:45.480
So this is the storage where the users are interacting all the time.

09:45.480 --> 09:47.360
So where do we find the user data?

09:47.360 --> 09:52.720
If you go to our database sales db and then you go to the tables, now we find all these tables that

09:52.720 --> 09:56.400
we are already used, the customers, employees, orders and so on.

09:56.720 --> 09:59.160
Those tables are the user data.

09:59.160 --> 10:07.040
So now if I go and say select from sales orders and all those informations that we are seeing now are

10:07.560 --> 10:08.720
the users data.

10:08.720 --> 10:11.400
So this is what we users actually care about.

10:11.400 --> 10:13.840
All other stuff that we see inside databases.

10:13.840 --> 10:15.520
As a user, we don't care about it.

10:15.560 --> 10:17.560
We care only about our data.

10:17.560 --> 10:20.520
But in the database we don't have only the user data.

10:20.520 --> 10:22.480
We have many other informations.

10:22.480 --> 10:25.640
So this is what we mean with the user data storage.

10:29.600 --> 10:31.320
Now what is system catalog.

10:31.320 --> 10:36.000
This is the internal storage for the database for its own information.

10:36.000 --> 10:41.650
So it's like a blueprint that keeps tracking Everything about the database itself.

10:41.650 --> 10:48.530
So that means the main purpose of the system catalog is that it holds the metadata information about

10:48.530 --> 10:49.410
the database.

10:49.410 --> 10:50.770
So what is a metadata?

10:50.810 --> 10:53.970
Metadata is data about data.

10:54.010 --> 10:55.930
Now let's understand what this means.

10:55.930 --> 10:59.490
What we have done so far is that we have created a table called customers.

10:59.530 --> 11:04.370
And we have defined inside it like multiple columns like the customer ID, first name, last name,

11:04.370 --> 11:07.330
and then we have inserted our data inside this table.

11:07.330 --> 11:09.890
So we have inserted five customers.

11:09.890 --> 11:12.130
So those are my data.

11:12.130 --> 11:15.930
I have created those informations and stored it inside the database.

11:16.170 --> 11:18.530
That's why we call it the user data.

11:18.530 --> 11:20.130
So nothing so far is new.

11:20.130 --> 11:26.050
So now what happens behind the scenes is that the database server will not only store the user data

11:26.050 --> 11:31.850
that you have provided, but also it's going to go and store a different type of data inside the database.

11:31.850 --> 11:34.490
And this data is the metadata.

11:34.490 --> 11:40.900
So the database server can store the Metadata of the customer's table and it can look like this.

11:40.900 --> 11:42.300
There is like a table name.

11:42.300 --> 11:48.100
There is a column names, and those are the column names that you have defined inside your database.

11:48.100 --> 11:53.660
And those are the column names that you have defined as you are creating the table customers.

11:53.660 --> 11:59.580
And it can store as well additional informations like which data type like the customer id is int and

11:59.580 --> 12:05.660
the last name is varchar, and many other informations like the length of the column and whether the

12:05.660 --> 12:07.340
column is nullable or not.

12:07.340 --> 12:13.660
So as you can see in the metadata, we are having a description, a data about the structure of the

12:13.660 --> 12:14.460
customers.

12:14.460 --> 12:19.820
And in the metadata we can find a lot of informations about not only the tables and columns, but as

12:19.820 --> 12:22.140
well about the schemas and the database.

12:22.140 --> 12:26.500
So you can find a full catalog about the structure of your database.

12:26.540 --> 12:28.420
Basic table, the customers table.

12:28.540 --> 12:31.620
It contains data about the actual data.

12:31.620 --> 12:35.990
So it stores data about the customers but the metadata of the of their customers.

12:35.990 --> 12:39.110
Table contains data about data.

12:39.110 --> 12:45.230
So in the databases, each table that you are using in order to store your data has a table twin that

12:45.230 --> 12:48.070
describes the structure of your data.

12:48.070 --> 12:51.870
So this is what we mean with a system catalog or a metadata.

12:51.910 --> 12:57.310
And now you might ask where I can find all those system catalog and metadata inside our client here.

12:57.310 --> 13:02.590
Well you cannot navigate through those informations in the Object Explorer like we used to do for the

13:02.590 --> 13:03.310
user data.

13:03.310 --> 13:09.750
But you can find those informations in a special hidden schema called the information schema.

13:09.790 --> 13:17.870
The information schema in SQL server is a system defined schema that contains a set of built in views

13:17.870 --> 13:24.070
that help us to find information about our database like tables, columns, and other objects.

13:24.070 --> 13:25.590
So let's go and explore it.

13:25.630 --> 13:31.590
We're going to go and say select star from Information Schema.

13:31.590 --> 13:32.950
And then let's have a dot.

13:32.990 --> 13:39.320
And now we get from SQL a list of all views that are available in order to browse the metadata of our

13:39.320 --> 13:40.040
database.

13:40.120 --> 13:46.160
So for example, you can see here tables, you can see informations about the views and as well about

13:46.160 --> 13:46.960
the columns.

13:46.960 --> 13:50.720
So let's go and select the columns and let's go and execute it.

13:50.760 --> 13:56.920
And now in the output we can find informations about the schema about the table names like for example

13:56.920 --> 13:58.200
here the customers.

13:58.240 --> 14:00.840
Let me just go and select this table.

14:00.840 --> 14:05.560
And then we find all the columns inside this table how they are sorted.

14:05.560 --> 14:13.000
So we have here the order of each column and as well the data type and the size of each column and many

14:13.000 --> 14:13.720
other stuff.

14:13.720 --> 14:19.600
So as you can see we got here all the informations, all the metadata of each table.

14:19.600 --> 14:22.600
And as well for each column inside the table.

14:22.600 --> 14:25.800
So with that you can check which tables does exist in your database.

14:25.800 --> 14:28.200
For example I find here like something called Test2.

14:28.360 --> 14:30.840
So maybe I was trying to test something.

14:30.880 --> 14:32.740
I can go now and clean up stuff.

14:32.740 --> 14:33.100
Right.

14:33.100 --> 14:36.780
And this is exactly why the database maintained such a catalog.

14:36.820 --> 14:42.660
It tells the database to quickly find the structure of each table and of each column, and it helps

14:42.660 --> 14:45.940
me, as well as a user, to browse the catalog of the database.

14:45.940 --> 14:51.100
So for example, I can go over here and say, okay, let's get a distinct table name.

14:51.980 --> 14:56.540
So with that, I will get a list of everything that I have inside the database.

14:56.540 --> 15:00.060
So we have the customers, employees and some tests that I have done.

15:00.060 --> 15:01.700
So metadata are awesome.

15:05.660 --> 15:09.300
Now we come to the third storage that temporary data storage.

15:09.300 --> 15:15.900
It is a temporary space used by the database for short term tasks like processing a query or sorting

15:15.900 --> 15:16.340
data.

15:16.340 --> 15:19.220
And once these tasks are done, what's going to happen?

15:19.260 --> 15:21.460
The database is going to go and clean up the storage.

15:21.500 --> 15:24.820
And now of course, the question is where we can find these temporary tables.

15:24.820 --> 15:27.380
That is using the temporary storage in the disk.

15:27.540 --> 15:30.070
Well actually if you go to the object Explorer.

15:30.110 --> 15:36.630
You will not find it inside our database sales DB, but you will find it inside the system databases.

15:36.670 --> 15:42.030
Now, since we are working locally, we have the full access to everything inside the SQL server.

15:42.030 --> 15:46.950
But in real projects, if you are just a user or let's say a developer, you will not have access to

15:46.950 --> 15:50.630
the system databases only for the database administrators.

15:50.630 --> 15:52.630
But now we are working on a local copy.

15:52.630 --> 15:55.150
So let's go to the system database.

15:55.150 --> 16:00.030
And here you have a special database from the SQL server called Tempdb.

16:00.150 --> 16:04.510
And if you go inside it we will find here tables and temporary tables.

16:04.510 --> 16:10.030
So this is exactly where you can find all the temporary tables that you are generating now currently

16:10.030 --> 16:12.110
we didn't create any temporary tables.

16:12.110 --> 16:13.350
That's why it's empty.

16:13.350 --> 16:18.910
But once you start creating temporary tables you will find those tables underneath this folder.

16:18.910 --> 16:22.430
We will learn about that temporary tables in the next sections.

16:26.470 --> 16:30.080
So these are the main component of the database architecture.

16:30.120 --> 16:31.280
So now let's have an example.

16:31.280 --> 16:35.400
Now we have a table called orders that is stored inside the user storage.

16:35.400 --> 16:38.240
And the metadata of this table is stored in the catalog.

16:38.240 --> 16:40.640
So now let's say that you are at the client side.

16:40.640 --> 16:44.200
And you write a simple Select query in order to select the data of the orders.

16:44.200 --> 16:47.680
So now that query is sent to the server in order to be executed.

16:47.680 --> 16:51.000
And the database engine can take the query in order to process it.

16:51.000 --> 16:55.080
So first the database engine can check whether we have the data in the cache.

16:55.080 --> 16:59.280
Because if the data is stored in the cache, then things are going to be really fast and the database

16:59.280 --> 17:01.000
engine can solve the task quickly.

17:01.000 --> 17:04.360
But in this scenario we don't have the orders information in the cache.

17:04.400 --> 17:08.760
That's why the database engine can say, okay, it's not in the cache, let's check the disk.

17:08.760 --> 17:13.160
So it will find the orders information in the disk and the query can be executed.

17:13.200 --> 17:18.520
Then the result of this query is going to be sent back to the client side where at the end.

17:18.520 --> 17:22.240
In return you will see in the output the result of the table orders.

17:22.240 --> 17:25.360
So this is how the SQL database execute.

17:25.400 --> 17:26.840
Very simple select query.
